Time moves like a river, but we often treat it like a snapshot. We freeze moments in our memories, 
forgetting that the banks are constantly changing shape. The true art of living lies not in holding onto the water,
but in learning how to float along with the current.
The old oak tree at the edge of the clearing has seen generations pass,
yet it never rushes its own growth. Each leaf catches the morning light exactly as it is meant to,
turning sunlight into silent strength. There is a quiet brilliance in simply standing your ground and weathering the seasons.
Progress is rarely a straight line. It is a series of loops, detours,
and sudden stops that eventually point you in the right direction.
Every small step forward, even the ones that feel insignificant,
builds the momentum needed to clear the next major hurdle.
